        { "id": "prt_06b", "sessionID": "ses_104", "messageID": "msg_06", "type": "reasoning", "text": "Separate the event loop / non-blocking idea from the multithreading idea clearly.", "time": { "start": 1758830022000, "end": 1758830023000 } },
        { "id": "prt_06c", "sessionID": "ses_104", "messageID": "msg_06", "type": "text", "text": "Close but not quite - non-blocking isn't the same as multithreaded. Your JS code still runs one line at a time on a single main thread. `async`/`await` lets that thread go do other work (like handling another request) while it waits for the DB response, instead of freezing - the DB query itself happens elsewhere (the database, or Node's internal I/O), but your JS callback that reads the result always runs back on that same single thread, one at a time via the event loop." },
        { "id": "prt_06d", "sessionID": "ses_104", "messageID": "msg_06", "type": "step-finish", "reason": "stop", "cost": 0.01, "tokens": { "input": 1300, "output": 340, "reasoning": 0, "cache": { "read": 0, "write": 0 } } }
